Consider the following code snippet that reads and prints the contents of a text file named `sample.txt` containing `"This is a sample text file."`:
```c
FILE *f_ptr = fopen("sample.txt", "r");
if (f_ptr == NULL) printf("Cannot open file");
char ch;
while ((ch = fgetc(f_ptr)) != EOF)
printf("%c", ch);
fclose(f_ptr);
```

Consider the following complete C program that reads data from a file containing `"Hello World\n42"`:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "r");
char buffer[100];
int number;
if (fp != NULL) {
fgets(buffer, 100, fp);
fscanf(fp, "%d", &number);
fclose(fp);
}
return 0;
}
```
What will be stored in the variables after execution?

Consider the following code snippet that writes text to a file named `output.txt`:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
FILE *file_ptr = fopen("output.txt", "w");
if (file_ptr == NULL) {
printf("Unable to open the file.");
return 1;
}
fprintf(file_ptr, "Hello, World!\nThis is a test.");
fclose(file_ptr);
return 0;
}
```
Assuming the code executed successfully, what will be the content of `output.txt`?
